# Refraction Pipeline & Chromatic Dispersion

The `refraction` material mode in `TxBaseSurface` and `TxGlassSurface` recreates physical refraction and dispersion inside modern Web and Electron runtimes.

## Snell's Law and Physical Refraction

Light traveling across media boundaries follows Snell's Law:
$$n_1 \sin\theta_1 = n_2 \sin\theta_2$$
When viewing background elements through a card, perimeter light should bend. TuffEx applies parameterized displacement vectors to shift background pixels, mimicking optical refraction.

## RGB Chromatic Dispersion

Natural white light comprises a continuous spectrum. Inside real prisms, different wavelengths exhibit distinct refractive indexes (Cauchy's dispersion equation):
$$n(\lambda) = A + \frac{B}{\lambda^2}$$
Blue light refracts more sharply than red light ($n_{\text{Blue}} > n_{\text{Red}}$). TuffEx introduces slight angular offsets across Red, Green, and Blue channels, separating subtle spectral fringes along refracted contours.

## Smoothstep Intensity Curve Mapping

To avoid abrupt transitions at low intensities and severe distortions at high values, TuffEx applies a cubic `smoothstep` curve:
$$S(x) = 3x^2 - 2x^3 \quad (x \in [0, 1])$$
This $C^1$-continuous Hermite interpolation eliminates boundary shocks, ensuring the refraction strength parameter behaves predictably.

## The SVG Displacement Map Pipeline

TuffEx implements its refraction shader via a GPU-accelerated SVG filter pipeline:

:::TuffCodeBlock{lang="xml"}
---
code: |
  <!-- TxGlassSurface Underlying Shader Pipeline -->
  <filter id="tx-refract-filter" x="-20%" y="-20%" width="140%" height="140%">
    <!-- Generate Perlin / fractal perturbation source -->
    <feTurbulence type="fractalNoise" baseFrequency="0.04" numOctaves="2" result="noise" />
    <!-- Pixel displacement mapping along Red/Green channels -->
    <feDisplacementMap in="SourceGraphic" in2="noise" scale="12" xChannelSelector="R" yChannelSelector="G" />
  </filter>
---
:::

The filter couples with CSS variables injected at runtime, allowing the GPU compositor to paint real-time optical frames.
